(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypotension-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Hypotension Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with cardiomyopathy has had pressures around 92/58 for many months. She has intermittent lightheadedness that comes and goes. How is her hypotension best classified?","choices":["Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Acute reflex hypotension","Chronic cardiogenic hypotension","Acute toxic hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic causes such as cardiomyopathy and reduced ejection fraction heart failure produce chronic hypotension, which waxes and wanes and is intermittently symptomatic. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome is started on fludrocortisone after water and salt alone did not control her symptoms. Which effect of the drug should she be warned about?","choices":["Loss of consciousness","Salt and water retention","Supine hypertension","Worsening kidney disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone causes salt and water retention, which is why it is avoided in congestive heart failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman standing at an outdoor event in the heat develops tunnel vision, lightheadedness and dizziness but is still conscious. If her pressure keeps falling, what is likely to follow?","choices":["A prolonged coma","Supine hypertension","A brief faint","A generalized seizure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms are near-syncope, which precedes syncope: a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with valve disease faints on exertion, and his echocardiogram confirms a structural cause. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["No referral is needed","Cardiology consultation","Neurology consultation","Endocrinology referral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Structural cardiogenic syncope is worked up with echocardiography or stress echocardiography together with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman had her diuretic dose increased 1 week ago for high readings. She now feels lightheaded whenever she stands and nearly fainted at church. Supine she is 132/78 with a heart rate of 70; standing she is 108/68 with a heart rate of 96. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic cardiogenic hypotension","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medication and volume loss are typical non-neurogenic causes, and the heart rate rise from 70 to 96 shows the autonomic reflex is intact. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man had a faint while urinating at night after an evening of drinking alcohol and little water. He takes an alpha-1 blocker and lisinopril. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Drink less water in the evening","Stand quickly to void at night","Start midodrine at bedtime","Limit alcohol and keep hydrated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol and relative dehydration predispose him to micturition syncope, and reflex hypotension is managed by reducing or removing the trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome has palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that ease when she lies down. Within 8 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 72 to 108 while her pressure stays steady. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A rise of more than 30 beats per minute within 10 minutes of standing, improving when lying down, in a young woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome fits the syndrome, sometimes expanded as positional. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has a pressure of 104/64 at a routine visit and feels well. She worries because it is lower than her usual readings. What should she be told about this reading?","choices":["One reading is a snapshot in time","Any reading this low needs pressors","Pressure norms are fixed for all ages","It proves autonomic nerve failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each reading is only a snapshot in time and is judged against age, comorbidities and the possibility of relative hypotension, together with symptoms. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has vomited for 2 days and fainted on standing. Supine he is 136/78 with a heart rate of 66; after 3 minutes standing he is 104/64 with a heart rate of 94. What does this heart rate response indicate?","choices":["An intact autonomic response","Failure of the autonomic system","A reflex surge in vagal tone","A primary ventricular arrhythmia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A heart rate rise from 66 to 94 as pressure falls shows an intact baroreceptor-mediated sympathetic response, which favors non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from multiple system atrophy has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). He remains symptomatic on standing despite slow position changes. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Slow position changes","Midodrine","Droxidopa","Fludrocortisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is best with neurogenic syncope but is avoided with supine hypertension, severe heart disease and chronic kidney disease. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman faints while having blood drawn. Just before, she felt warm, nauseated and sweaty, and she recovered fully within a minute of lying down. Her examination and standing readings afterward are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Situational reflex syncope","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fear and pain are vasovagal triggers, a prodrome of warmth, nausea and sweating precedes it, and it is most common in young women; recovery is rapid and complete. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after taking a large quantity of a relative's blood pressure pills. His pressure dropped suddenly over the past hour and he is dizzy. How is his hypotension best classified?","choices":["Chronic hypotension from a cardiac cause","Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Acute hypotension from a toxic cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medication-mediated overdose is a toxic cause of acute hypotension, which is of sudden onset, usually symptomatic and tied to a specific trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man reports fainting twice after standing. Before any test is ordered, which part of his evaluation is most important for identifying the source?","choices":["A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","A chest radiograph","A detailed history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of accurately diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic autonomic neuropathy still faints on standing despite slow position changes. Her standing pressure falls 32 mmHg with almost no heart rate rise. She has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), heart disease or kidney disease. Which medication is most appropriate?","choices":["Norepinephrine","Midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is best with neurogenic syncope, and none of the settings in which it is avoided, supine hypertension, severe heart disease or chronic kidney disease, applies to her. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has recurrent lightheadedness on standing. Her orthostatic readings meet criteria, but her history does not make clear whether the cause is autonomic failure or something else. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her and stop evaluating","Repeat readings until they normalize","Refer to the right specialty to confirm","Start a vasopressor in clinic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important tool for the source of orthostatic syncope, or for referring to the right specialty for confirmation when it remains unclear. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vasovagal syncope must have regular blood draws for a chronic illness and has fainted at two of them. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Take midodrine before each draw","Fast and restrict fluids beforehand","Take safety precautions during draws","Stop the blood draws altogether"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When a trigger cannot be avoided, safety precautions during the trigger are recommended, alongside trigger avoidance wherever possible. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man fainted at home and has recovered. His electrocardiogram and glucose are normal. Which tests look for an infectious or metabolic contributor?","choices":["Orthostatic vitals only","A blood count and metabolic panel","A tilt-table test and a log","An echocardiogram and stress test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unremarkable blood counts and a metabolic panel lower suspicion of an infectious or metabolic source, as glucose and the electrocardiogram address hypoglycemia and dysrhythmia. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a known aortic aneurysm faints suddenly in the clinic waiting room and now has severe chest pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["An outpatient echocardiogram next week","A symptom log for a month","Orthostatic vitals and reassurance","Emergent testing in the emergency department"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vascular causes of cardiogenic syncope, dissection, aneurysm, stroke and pulmonary embolism, call for emergent testing in the emergency department plus cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man on a newly increased diuretic keeps standing up quickly despite advice, and his pressure falls 30 mmHg on standing. If this continues, what is he most at risk of?","choices":["Supine hypertension overnight","A seizure disorder","Diabetic autonomic neuropathy","Syncope from cerebral hypoperfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is the result of symptomatic hypotension: when the standing pressure falls far enough, brain perfusion drops and consciousness is briefly lost. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from volume loss is lightheaded on standing in the clinic but alert, with a standing pressure of 100/62. Which treatment is inappropriate in this setting?","choices":["A norepinephrine infusion","Correcting the volume loss","Slow position changes","Reviewing her medications"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). Lying down she is 176/96; after 3 minutes standing she is 152/90, and she feels mildly lightheaded. Do these readings meet the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["No; readings need 10 minutes","No; a fall over 30 mmHg is needed","Yes; the fall exceeds 20 mmHg","Yes; any fall with symptoms counts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With an elevated baseline or supine hypertension the systolic fall must exceed 30 mmHg; hers is 24 and the diastolic fall is only 6. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with hypertension takes lisinopril, metoprolol and a diuretic added last month. She has lost weight, her pressure is now 98/62, and she is dizzy on standing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start a vasopressor infusion","Refer for a tilt-table test","Reduce her blood pressure medicines","Add midodrine to her medicines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When hypotension is iatrogenic, the offending agent is reduced or removed; her antihypertensives now overshoot as her weight and pressure have fallen. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction heart failure faints abruptly while walking after a brief racing heartbeat. After his electrocardiogram, which further test helps capture an intermittent arrhythmia?","choices":["Cardiac monitoring","A tilt-table test","A patient log alone","Orthostatic vitals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The arrhythmia work-up is an electrocardiogram, cardiac monitoring and a stress test, together with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has palpitations and dizziness on standing that improve when lying down, and her standing heart rate climbs well above baseline without a fall in pressure. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["An echocardiogram","A stress test","An episode log","A tilt-table test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The tilt-table test is the listed diagnostic test for the syndrome, with a sensitivity of about 40% and specificity above 80%. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from diabetes also has chronic kidney disease. She remains symptomatic despite slow position changes. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Fludrocortisone","Slow position changes","Midodrine","Droxidopa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic kidney disease is one of the listed settings, with supine hypertension and severe heart disease, in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and numb feet has a 30 mmHg standing pressure fall with a heart rate rise of only 3 beats and no sign of dehydration. What is the most likely mechanism of her syncope?","choices":["A vagal surge with vasodilation","Reduced volume from fluid loss","A fast rhythm reducing output","Inadequate norepinephrine release"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autonomic dysfunction leaves too little norepinephrine released from sympathetic postganglionic neurons, so vessels fail to constrict and the heart rate rises less than expected. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with diabetic autonomic neuropathy has recently begun fainting on standing. She has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), heart disease or kidney disease. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Slow position changes, treat the cause","A vasopressor infusion in clinic","Fludrocortisone before anything else","Strict fluid restriction each day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes and treatment of the underlying condition are first-line for orthostatic syncope; drugs come in when these are not enough. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man's pressure is 100/64, which is normal for many adults but far below his usual 150/90, and he now feels lightheaded. How should this reading be interpreted?","choices":["As relative hypotension for him","As normal, since it is above 90","As a measurement artifact","As proof of autonomic failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a fall below normal, and normal is judged against the patient's age, comorbidities and own baseline, so a relative fall can be significant. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from diabetes asks what she can do herself to stop fainting when she gets out of bed. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Drink less water in the evening","Stay in bed until symptoms stop","Change position slowly when rising","Stand quickly to train her reflexes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, with a pause sitting before standing, alongside treatment of the underlying cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has fainted three times in a year, each time after standing for a long while in the heat, always after feeling warm and nauseated. Her examination and standing readings are normal. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Trigger avoidance","Daily midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reflex syncope is treated by avoiding its triggers, with safety precautions when a trigger cannot be avoided; drugs are not first-line. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
